            "review_title": "Review at 94 Restaurant",
            "review_text": "LamN\nSingapore, Singapore4905 contributions\n2\nAfter your nap, grab a cab to go to this drab place; the crab is fab with a beer on tap!\nFeb 2018 • Couples\nQuite commonly in Asia, there are lots of imitations/fakes! For example, in Hanoi, on the same street, there are a dozen dog meat restaurants all called Anh Tú or a variation thereof!\n\nThe story of the two crabmeat restaurants in HCM is as follows: In 1992, an eatery specialising on crabmeat was started by Madame Thúy on the ground floor of 94 Đinh Tiên Hoàng street. In 1994, its lease was not renewed, and it moved a few houses away, to 84 Đinh Tiên Hoàng street. It now trades as “Quán Thúy”, with the subtitle “Quán 94 cũ” (ie. “formerly or old 94 restaurant”). In 1994, the landlord of 94 DTH started a crabmeat eatery with exactly the same menu. It now trades as “Quán 94 gốc” (ie. “original 94 restaurant”), with the subtitle “không chi nhánh” (ie. “no branch/affiliate”)!! Both are equally modest-looking, and pricing is similar - excellent value by international standards (this is crabmeat after all), but quite pricey for Vietnam, although lots of locals eat here. \n\nEach has its fans, but we have been customers of the “copycat” one (at 94 DTH) for years, and believe it is more refined. All the crab dishes here are excellent, except for the soft shell crab (where the heavy batter is too overwhelming) and the soup (a bit too starchy). Best are the crab spring rolls, crab glass noodles, and crab fried rice. They only use crabmeat in spring rolls, whereas the old one at 84 DTH add pork (in their soups too), so Muslim customers should be careful.\nRead more\nWritten February 9, 2018",
            "rating": 5.0,
            "review_date": "2026-10-13",
            "travel_date": "Recent",
            "feature_depth_status": "Schema-Full-Features-Validated"
        },
